bound that loosens with the sample so that its bias shrinks. Whether the
rule carries over to the density-ratio weights of a continuous exposure has
not been established in the literature. Here \eqn{n} counts the weights that
are present, so for weights built from a trimmed propensity score, whose
trimmed units are \code{NA}, it is the number of units kept by the trim. The
bound is below 1 for \eqn{n \le 6} (0.72 at \eqn{n = 5}), so for a sample
that small \code{"adaptive"} pulls every weight above that value down to a
constant below 1.

\code{"wt"} winsorizes at the values given, and \code{"count"} at the order statistic
next to the weights it bounds. Under \code{"count"}, a weight tied with that
order statistic is not moved, so fewer weights than \code{upper} may change. The
two counts must leave at least two weights between them, and \code{upper} alone
must leave at least one weight below it.

\code{"pctl"} reads its bounds with \code{\link[stats:quantile]{stats::quantile()}} at its default type, as
